WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.767
Secrets are high-risk configuration values.

2
00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:14.415
They include database credentials, API keys, certificates, private keys, signing keys, encryption keys, service tokens, storage credentials, webhook secrets, and connection strings.

3
00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:23.939
Even harmless placeholder names such as ExampleApiKey or ExampleConnectionString should remind teams to keep real values out of ordinary code paths.

4
00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:33.399
Secret values should not be stored in source code, appsettings files, build logs, crash dumps, screenshots, support bundles, or ordinary telemetry.

5
00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:40.800
These locations are copied, searched, retained, and shared in ways that make accidental disclosure hard to contain.

6
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:47.235
Secret handling should assume that convenience copies will eventually escape their original context.

7
00:00:47.235 --> 00:00:57.210
Use supported secret-management mechanisms, managed identities, deployment secrets, platform secret stores, or external secret providers where appropriate.

8
00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:09.051
The best choice depends on the hosting model, but the principle is consistent: the application should receive only the access it needs, through a channel designed for sensitive values.

9
00:01:09.051 --> 00:01:12.011
Separate secret references from secret values.

10
00:01:12.011 --> 00:01:23.209
A configuration value may safely name ExampleCertificateName or ExampleStorageAccount when it is only a reference, while the actual key material remains in a protected store.

11
00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:28.936
This distinction helps teams document configuration without publishing the secret itself.

12
00:01:28.936 --> 00:01:36.015
Secret rotation, ownership, access control, and emergency revocation should be planned before they are needed.

13
00:01:36.015 --> 00:01:47.406
Teams should know who owns each secret, which applications use it, which identities can read it, how it rotates, how it can be revoked, and how to recover if a value is exposed.

14
00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:53.005
Treat connection strings as sensitive even when they do not visibly contain a password.

15
00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:59.054
They can reveal internal names, provider details, account relationships, or trust assumptions.

16
00:01:59.054 --> 00:02:08.321
In many environments they also enable access through managed identity or integrated authentication, so they still deserve protection and review.
